*Feature Translation: When the Cloud Ecosystem Enters a Governance Phase
<https://substack.com/redirect/1caae3db-46d3-446b-9ae0-90d16b6fdbb4?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*

*Context: *Today, friends, we are learning about *commission rebates* [=E8=
=BF=94=E4=BD=A3]
in China=E2=80=99s cloud computing ecosystem. How=E2=80=99s that for a lede=
? First,
however, we need to understand a key player: reseller partners that sell
cloud services from big providers. In the case of *AWS resellers
<https://substack.com/redirect/705f0f86-cbbe-4088-8fe4-dcf0b1713c21?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
:

*Resellers purchase AWS services in bulk at discounted rates and then
resell them to customers. They usually bundle AWS offerings with additional
services such as a cost visibility platform, consulting, support, and
managed services, so the final bundle seems very lucrative to the end
users. The target segment for AWS resellers typically are businesses
looking for more personalized and tailored cloud solutions.*

Now, these resellers make money off of *commission
<https://substack.com/redirect/c7f42c29-5594-4163-ae33-03726a2411a2?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*.
Alibaba Cloud or Huawei Cloud will give resellers a commission (perhaps,
20%) for each new customer they recruit. This brings us back to the concept
of *commission rebates*. In China=E2=80=99s cloud market =E2=80=9Cwhere new=
 customers are
hard to find=E2=80=9D (per this week=E2=80=99s *AItechtalk article
<https://substack.com/redirect/25866a40-d5fc-43a5-9096-1a3150e58a8c?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*),
resellers are giving a portion of their commission back to new customers,
in order to attract more sales.

*Key Takeaways: Race-to-the-bottom competition in commission rebates have
sown chaos in the cloud computing market.*

   -

   This week=E2=80=99s feature translation gives examples of rebates regula=
rly
   reaching 60 to 70% and even as high as 83% in one case.
   -

   By giving up so much of their commission, these sales agents and
   resellers are making a bet that is very risky for their own companies (a=
nd
   one that=E2=80=99s not sustainable for the overall ecosystem): use extre=
mely
   discounted offerings to gain a bunch of customers and survive on incenti=
ves
   that the cloud vendor provides for increased sales OR go bankrupt.

*This reliance on short-term arbitrage is not sustainable for a future
AI-centered ecosystem. The article warns, =E2=80=9CBefore fully rushing tow=
ards the
future, cloud vendors must first clean up the chaos accumulated in the past
cloud ecosystem.=E2=80=9D Let=E2=80=99s scrutinize how this unscrupulous pr=
ice war creates
perverse incentives for each part of the ecosystem:*

   -

   The customer only cares about price, as opposed to the actual
   performance differences between different providers. =E2=80=9CSome agent=
s have
   found that many customers believe that cloud service providers should of=
fer
   rebates of over 50%,=E2=80=9D AItechtalk reports.
   -

   The partner vendors do not make the long-term investments needed to
   adapt to an AI era. The article explains, =E2=80=9C*The essence of AI pr=
ojects
   is complex value creation, requiring partners to possess deep industry
   knowledge, capabilities to provide solutions, and sustained resilience o=
f
   services. Continuing to use the =E2=80=98sales is king=E2=80=99 logic of=
 the old era will
   only exclude the most capable partners best suited for AI transformation=
,
   or force them back into the quagmire of price wars*.=E2=80=9D
   -

      Lin Fei, a sales agent tells AItechtalk, =E2=80=9C*We diligently work=
 on
      earnings, but our commissions are lower than those who focus on clien=
t
      relationships*.=E2=80=9D The implication here, I think, is that other=
 agents
      and partners who prioritize building customized services and
products that
      meet their client=E2=80=99s needs can=E2=80=99t get as much business =
because they are
      offering lower prices by giving up more of their commission.

   -

   Finally, in their obsession with market share, the large cloud providers
   neglect genuine collaborations with their partners.

*What does cleaning up this ecosystem look like? Here are just a few signs
that China=E2=80=99s cloud market may be entering a governance phase: integ=
rity
oaths, multiple levels of approvals for agents to conduct in-person visits,
and a rectification campaign.*

   -

   Last month, Huawei Cloud held a ceremony to launch its sales partner
   policy. Over 1,300 partner representatives took an =E2=80=9Cintegrity oa=
th,=E2=80=9D
   promising to not use one=E2=80=99s guanxi relationships to seek improper=
 benefits.

<https://substack.com/redirect/4b6f89c4-117a-47d7-b0df-b2170e890c2a?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Source:
AItechtalk [AI=E7=A7=91=E6=8A=80=E8=AF=84=E8=AE=BA]

   -

   Some large cloud computing providers have conducted internal
   anti-corruption campaigns, replacing heads of procurement and punishing
   agents who false inflated earnings.
   -

   Other firms have removed agents with family connections from their
   system and required suppliers and procurement personnel to communicate
   through company emails.
   -

   To me, the effectiveness of initiatives like this are more meaningful to
   follow than model performance or energy budgets. When it comes to China=
=E2=80=99s
   AI diffusion, whether the ecosystem encourages sustainable adoption=E2=
=80=94 rather
   than, say, one-off, unscalable *all-in-one machines
   <https://substack.com/redirect/dec714ff-8078-4bcb-a10c-6fbf60758124?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
   that work well for press releases =E2=80=94 is what matters. As the arti=
cle points
   out: =E2=80=9C*In a long-distance race, a clean ecosystem is more crucia=
l than
   short-term sprints in determining future growth potential*.=E2=80=9D

*FULL TRANSLATION: Ferocious AI Competition, Partners=E2=80=99 Anxiety: Whe=
n the
Cloud Ecosystem Enters a Governance Phase
<https://substack.com/redirect/1caae3db-46d3-446b-9ae0-90d16b6fdbb4?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
*ChinAI Links (One to Occupy your Attention)**Must-read: Purdue effectively
bans grad students from China, other countries, faculty say
<https://substack.com/redirect/7c567c3e-8db1-4462-b867-d7f1c82cd3dc?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*

For *Lafayette Journal & Courier*, Seth Nelson reports on a troubling
development: =E2=80=9CPurdue University administrators are reportedly press=
uring
faculty to reject graduate school applicants from China and other
=E2=80=98adversary nations.=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D He reports:

*In a mid-October meeting, the Department of Statistics=E2=80=99 graduate a=
dviser
suggested to faculty that they might have to begin turning away a large
number of students at the door, one professor in the department said.*

*=E2=80=9CThey said we shouldn=E2=80=99t tell Chinese students they wouldn=
=E2=80=99t be admitted,=E2=80=9D
they said. =E2=80=9CWe were just encouraged to find anybody else from anywh=
ere
except China.=E2=80=9D*

These types of policies (and the chilling effects they create) will do far
more harm to U.S. technology leadership than anything related to chips.
Yet, the latter gets way more political capital and attention.

What=E2=80=99s at stake here isn=E2=80=99t just technological leadership. I=
=E2=80=99m haunted by
the parallels to the German education system=E2=80=99s rejection of foreign
students prior to World War I. Here=E2=80=99s Wolfgang K=C3=B6nig=E2=80=99s=
 account (emphasis
mine):

Beginning in the 1890s, complaints against foreign students from some
segments of the German public, and from fellow students, increased
continuously until World War I. Behind these was a growing chauvinistic
resentment. *Some protestors feared that foreign electrical engineering
students would transfer knowledge to their home countries and therefore
enable their industries to compete successfully with Germany in the world
market*. Although industrialists, engineers, and engineering professors
rejected these attacks, German technische Hochschulen and state
governments, especially in Prussia, started to restrict the number of
foreign students. After 1905 the numbers and percentages of foreign
electrical engineering students decreased considerably.

Is this the type of society we want to follow in the footsteps of?
